{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's claim asserts resumption 'is safe only if the system emitted an explicit barrier or suspend event' — a necessary condition. But its own counterconditions identify two rival criteria (stateless self-contained events, and full-state checkpointing on every event) under which no barrier is needed and resumption is safe. The problem demands adjudication between rival criteria to determine 'exactly when' resumption is safe vs. refused. SRC_001 never reconciles the barrier gate with these alternatives: it presents a single universal necessary condition while simultaneously conceding it is unnecessary in acknowledged-safe cases. For a stateless system interrupted after a complete event with no barrier emitted, the main claim says 'must refuse' while the countercondition says 'safe' — a direct contradiction that prevents the target from determining exactly when resumption is permitted.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Stateless reasoning run; each event self-contained; token-budget exhaustion occurs immediately after a complete event; no barrier/suspend event was emitted.","claim_verdict":"Refuse resumption (barrier absent).","countercondition_verdict":"Safe to resume (stateless, complete event).","result":"Contradictory verdicts for identical scenario; target cannot determine exactly when resumption is safe."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 requires an exact causal fingerprint match (program_hash, input_hash, model_checkpoint_hash) at resumption equal to run start, and says any divergence means resumption must stay refused. Its own countercondition notes that for stochastic (nondeterministic) sampling — the dominant regime for LLM reasoning runs — 'exact fingerprint matching is impossible and the criterion would forbid all resumption, which is too strict.' The target identifies this rival criterion but never adjudicates it: it neither relaxes the fingerprint rule for stochastic systems nor explains how model_checkpoint_hash should be defined to accommodate nondeterminism. Consequently, for the primary class of token-budget-exhausted reasoning runs (temperature>0 LLM inference), the target can only output 'refuse always,' which it itself labels too strict. It thus fails to determine exactly when resumption is safe for stochastic runs, leaving the problem's core question unanswered for that regime.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"LLM reasoning run with temperature=0.7; token-budget exhaustion mid-run; at resumption time the nondeterministic sampling/RNG state differs from start-time state, so model_checkpoint_hash cannot match.","claim_verdict":"Refuse resumption (fingerprint diverges).","countercondition_verdict":"Criterion is too strict; should not forbid all resumption.","result":"No determinable safe-resumption condition for stochastic runs; target fails to adjudicate the rival criterion it itself raises."}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}